# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Python ---
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# ---------------------------------------------------- build output --------
# Everything here is produced from tracked sources. None of it is an input.
build/
dist/
release/
*.spec

# ------------------------------------------------- local user content -----
# The firmware directory is an operator choice made in Settings at runtime,
# and the images in it are the manufacturer's. It is not the firmware set for
# every OmniSuite user, it must never be committed, and it must never enter a
# build: adding it once produced a 981 MB executable, 908 MB of it firmware.
# The application ships the CODE that browses an operator-chosen folder; it
# does not ship the folder.
firmware/

# Development history kept on this machine, not published. See archive/README.md.
archive/

# Working material, device web-app bundles and QA baselines captured from the
# bench. Carries real addresses and third-party code; it is evidence, not source.
artifacts/

upgrademanager.zip

# ------------------------------------------- runtime / generated state ----
# Runtime configuration: holds device credentials and is written by the
# application into its data directory. The repository copy is never read --
# the server loads DATA_DIR/config.json -- and tracking it means the next
# password change gets committed. Start from config.example.json.
config.json

# Runtime caches. These are written by a running installation and carry the
# bench's addresses, MACs, hostnames and stored device passwords. The
# committed copies held only the documented default password, but the next
# scan against a fleet with a real one would have committed that. The server
# reads these from DATA_DIR, never from the repository.
units_cache.json
units_cache*.json
scan_results.json
units_view.csv

# Runtime acknowledgements and preferences, written per installation.
notice_ack.json
topology_ack.json
scan_preferences.json

# Logs, downloaded device logs and support bundles. A .vdf is an encrypted
# device log pulled from real hardware.
*.log
*.vdf
ts_dump*.zip

# Packaged-artifact smoke tests extract the application to a temp directory.
omnisuite-smoke-*/

# ------------------------------------------------ editor / machine --------
*.code-workspace
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
